-- title:
--   Chaining estimate for a single pair along a walk
-- statement:
--   Fix $d \in \mathbb{N}$ and a configuration $\Gamma$ on $\mathbb{R}^d$, i.e. an assignment of a double cone $\Gamma(z)$ to every point $z$. Let $\alpha, \Lambda, R_0, \lambda \in \mathbb{R}$ and let
--   $$\omega : \mathbb{R}^d \times \mathbb{R}^d \to [0,\infty]$$
--   be a jump measure on pairs of points. Assume:
--
--   1. $\omega$ satisfies the discrete kernel bounds $\mathrm{DiscreteKernelBounds}(\Gamma,\alpha,\Lambda,R_0,\mathbb{Z}^d,\omega)$ — the paper's assumption (4) restricted to the lattice: $\Lambda \ge 1$, $\omega$ is symmetric on $\mathbb{Z}^d$, and for lattice points $x, y$ at distance greater than $R_0$ the *two-sided* bound
--   $$\Lambda^{-1}\bigl(\mathbf{1}_{V^\Gamma[x]}(y) + \mathbf{1}_{V^\Gamma[y]}(x)\bigr)\,k_\alpha(x,y) \;\le\; \omega(x,y) \;\le\; \Lambda\,k_\alpha(x,y)$$
--   holds; in particular, as soon as *one* of the two points lies in the other's cone, $k_\alpha(x,y) \le \Lambda\,\omega(x,y)$;
--   2. $\alpha > 0$;
--   3. $\lambda \ge 1$.
--
--   Let $f : \mathbb{R}^d \to \mathbb{R}$ be an arbitrary function, let $x, y \in \mathbb{R}^d$, and let $w$ be a walk from $x$ to $y$ in the lattice graph $G$ attached to $\Gamma$ (vertices the lattice points, an edge between two points when one lies in the other's cone). Write $\ell(w)$ for the number of edges of $w$ and let its oriented edges (darts) be $(z_i, z_{i+1})$. Assume finally that every dart of $w$ satisfies both
--   $$\lVert z_i - z_{i+1}\rVert \le \lambda \lVert x - y\rVert \qquad\text{and}\qquad \lVert z_i - z_{i+1}\rVert > R_0 .$$
--
--   Then, writing $k_\alpha(x,y) = \lVert x-y\rVert^{-d-\alpha}$ for the $\alpha$-stable jump kernel,
--
--   $$\bigl(f(x) - f(y)\bigr)^2\, k_\alpha(x,y) \;\le\; \ell(w)\,\lambda^{\,d+\alpha}\,\Lambda \;\sum_{i} \bigl(f(z_i) - f(z_{i+1})\bigr)^2\, \omega(z_i, z_{i+1}),$$
--
--   the sum running over the darts of $w$ in order.
--
--   This is the displayed computation of Section 6 for a single pair: Cauchy–Schwarz converts the squared increment across the pair into $\ell(w)$ times the sum of squared increments along the walk, the upper bound on edge lengths converts $\lVert x-y\rVert^{-d-\alpha}$ into $\lambda^{d+\alpha}$ times the edge kernel, and the lower bound $\lVert z_i - z_{i+1}\rVert > R_0$ is exactly what makes the kernel comparison applicable at every edge. Summing this over all pairs, with the multiplicity bound controlling how often a given edge is reused, is what yields the comparison of quadratic forms.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** All quantities live in $\mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}^{\infty}$: the real quantities $(f(x)-f(y))^2$ and $\ell(w)\lambda^{d+\alpha}\Lambda$ enter through `ENNReal.ofReal`, which truncates negative reals to $0$, and the exponent $d + \alpha$ is a real exponent. The right-hand sum is a sum over the walk's *list* of darts, so an edge traversed several times contributes once per traversal. No measurability or regularity is required of $f$ or $\omega$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/dbenbenn/quadratic-forms-sobolev/blob/7a1a680db2124d46ce370c91fd450aa454edf491/QuadraticFormsSobolev/Section6.lean#L145-L244

